/*!

@page moving_guide Moving from RBLXWIN 2 to 3

@tableofcontents

This is a transition guide for moving from RBLXWIN 2 to 3.  It describes what has
changed or been removed, but does _not_ include
[new features](@ref news) unless they are required when moving an existing code
base onto the new API.  For example, the new multi-monitor functions are
required to create full screen windows with RBLXWIN 3.


@section moving_removed Changed and removed features

@subsection moving_renamed_files Renamed library and header file

The RBLXWIN 3 header is named @ref rblxwin.h and moved to the `RBLXWIN` directory, to
avoid collisions with the headers of other major versions.  Similarly, the RBLXWIN
3 library is named `rblxwin,` except when it's installed as a shared library on
Unix-like systems, where it uses the
[soname](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/soname) `librblxwin.so.3`.

@par Old syntax
@code
#include <GL/rblxwin.h>
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
#include <RBLXWIN/rblxwin.h>
@endcode


@subsection moving_threads Removal of threading functions

The threading functions have been removed, including the per-thread sleep
function.  They were fairly primitive, under-used, poorly integrated and took
time away from the focus of RBLXWIN (i.e.  context, input and window).  There are
better threading libraries available and native threading support is available
in both [C++11](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/thread) and
[C11](https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/thread), both of which are gaining
traction.

If you wish to use the C++11 or C11 facilities but your compiler doesn't yet
support them, see the
[TinyThread++](https://gitorious.org/tinythread/tinythreadpp) and
[TinyCThread](https://github.com/tinycthread/tinycthread) projects created by
the original author of RBLXWIN.  These libraries implement a usable subset of the
threading APIs in C++11 and C11, and in fact some RBLXWIN 3 test programs use
TinyCThread.

However, RBLXWIN 3 has better support for _use from multiple threads_ than RBLXWIN
2 had.  Contexts can be made current on any thread, although only a single
thread at a time, and the documentation explicitly states which functions may be
used from any thread and which must only be used from the main thread.

@par Removed functions
`rblxwinSleep`, `rblxwinCreateThread`, `rblxwinDestroyThread`, `rblxwinWaitThread`,
`rblxwinGetThreadID`, `rblxwinCreateMutex`, `rblxwinDestroyMutex`, `rblxwinLockMutex`,
`rblxwinUnlockMutex`, `rblxwinCreateCond`, `rblxwinDestroyCond`, `rblxwinWaitCond`,
`rblxwinSignalCond`, `rblxwinBroadcastCond` and `rblxwinGetNumberOfProcessors`.

@par Removed types
`RBLXWINthreadfun`


@subsection moving_image Removal of image and texture loading

The image and texture loading functions have been removed.  They only supported
the Targa image format, making them mostly useful for beginner level examples.
To become of sufficiently high quality to warrant keeping them in RBLXWIN 3, they
would need not only to support other formats, but also modern extensions to
OpenGL texturing.  This would either add a number of external
dependencies (libjpeg, libpng, etc.), or force RBLXWIN to ship with inline versions
of these libraries.

As there already are libraries doing this, it is unnecessary both to duplicate
the work and to tie the duplicate to RBLXWIN.  The resulting library would also be
platform-independent, as both OpenGL and stdio are available wherever RBLXWIN is.

@par Removed functions
`rblxwinReadImage`, `rblxwinReadMemoryImage`, `rblxwinFreeImage`, `rblxwinLoadTexture2D`,
`rblxwinLoadMemoryTexture2D` and `rblxwinLoadTextureImage2D`.


@subsection moving_stdcall Removal of RBLXWINCALL macro

The `RBLXWINCALL` macro, which made callback functions use
[__stdcall](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/zxk0tw93.aspx) on Windows,
has been removed.  RBLXWIN is written in C, not Pascal.  Removing this macro means
there's one less thing for application programmers to remember, i.e. the
requirement to mark all callback functions with `RBLXWINCALL`.  It also simplifies
the creation of DLLs and DLL link libraries, as there's no need to explicitly
disable `@n` entry point suffixes.

@par Old syntax
@code
void RBLXWINCALL callback_function(...);
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
void callback_function(...);
@endcode


@subsection moving_window_handles Window handle parameters

Because RBLXWIN 3 supports multiple windows, window handle parameters have been
added to all window-related RBLXWIN functions and callbacks.  The handle of
a newly created window is returned by @ref rblxwinCreateWindow (formerly
`rblxwinOpenWindow`).  Window handles are pointers to the
[opaque](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opaque_data_type) type @ref RBLXWINwindow.

@par Old syntax
@code
rblxwinSetWindowTitle("New Window Title");
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
rblxwinSetWindowTitle(window, "New Window Title");
@endcode


@subsection moving_monitor Explicit monitor selection

RBLXWIN 3 provides support for multiple monitors.  To request a full screen mode window,
instead of passing `RBLXWIN_FULLSCREEN` you specify which monitor you wish the
window to use.  The @ref rblxwinGetPrimaryMonitor function returns the monitor that
RBLXWIN 2 would have selected, but there are many other
[monitor functions](@ref monitor_guide).  Monitor handles are pointers to the
[opaque](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opaque_data_type) type @ref RBLXWINmonitor.

@par Old basic full screen
@code
rblxwinOpenWindow(640, 480, 8, 8, 8, 0, 24, 0, RBLXWIN_FULLSCREEN);
@endcode

@par New basic full screen
@code
window = rblxwinCreateWindow(640, 480, "My Window", rblxwinGetPrimaryMonitor(), NULL);
@endcode

@note The framebuffer bit depth parameters of `rblxwinOpenWindow` have been turned
into [window hints](@ref window_hints), but as they have been given
[sane defaults](@ref window_hints_values) you rarely need to set these hints.


@subsection moving_autopoll Removal of automatic event polling

RBLXWIN 3 does not automatically poll for events in @ref rblxwinSwapBuffers, meaning
you need to call @ref rblxwinPollEvents or @ref rblxwinWaitEvents yourself.  Unlike
buffer swap, which acts on a single window, the event processing functions act
on all windows at once.

@par Old basic main loop
@code
while (...)
{
    // Process input
    // Render output
    rblxwinSwapBuffers();
}
@endcode

@par New basic main loop
@code
while (...)
{
    // Process input
    // Render output
    rblxwinSwapBuffers(window);
    rblxwinPollEvents();
}
@endcode


@subsection moving_context Explicit context management

Each RBLXWIN 3 window has its own OpenGL context and only you, the application
programmer, can know which context should be current on which thread at any
given time.  Therefore, RBLXWIN 3 leaves that decision to you.

This means that you need to call @ref rblxwinMakeContextCurrent after creating
a window before you can call any OpenGL functions.


@subsection moving_hidpi Separation of window and framebuffer sizes

Window positions and sizes now use screen coordinates, which may not be the same
as pixels on machines with high-DPI monitors.  This is important as OpenGL uses
pixels, not screen coordinates.  For example, the rectangle specified with
`glViewport` needs to use pixels.  Therefore, framebuffer size functions have
been added.  You can retrieve the size of the framebuffer of a window with @ref
rblxwinGetFramebufferSize function.  A framebuffer size callback has also been
added, which can be set with @ref rblxwinSetFramebufferSizeCallback.

@par Old basic viewport setup
@code
rblxwinGetWindowSize(&width, &height);
glViewport(0, 0, width, height);
@endcode

@par New basic viewport setup
@code
rblxwinGetFramebufferSize(window, &width, &height);
glViewport(0, 0, width, height);
@endcode


@subsection moving_window_close Window closing changes

The `RBLXWIN_OPENED` window parameter has been removed.  As long as the window has
not been destroyed, whether through @ref rblxwinDestroyWindow or @ref
rblxwinTerminate, the window is "open".

A user attempting to close a window is now just an event like any other.  Unlike
RBLXWIN 2, windows and contexts created with RBLXWIN 3 will never be destroyed unless
you choose them to be.  Each window now has a close flag that is set to
`RBLXWIN_TRUE` when the user attempts to close that window.  By default, nothing else
happens and the window stays visible.  It is then up to you to either destroy
the window, take some other action or ignore the request.

You can query the close flag at any time with @ref rblxwinWindowShouldClose and set
it at any time with @ref rblxwinSetWindowShouldClose.

@par Old basic main loop
@code
while (rblxwinGetWindowParam(RBLXWIN_OPENED))
{
    ...
}
@endcode

@par New basic main loop
@code
while (!rblxwinWindowShouldClose(window))
{
    ...
}
@endcode

The close callback no longer returns a value.  Instead, it is called after the
close flag has been set so it can override its value, if it chooses to, before
event processing completes.  You may however not call @ref rblxwinDestroyWindow
from the close callback (or any other window related callback).

@par Old syntax
@code
int RBLXWINCALL window_close_callback(void);
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
void window_close_callback(RBLXWINwindow* window);
@endcode

@note RBLXWIN never clears the close flag to `RBLXWIN_FALSE`, meaning you can use it
for other reasons to close the window as well, for example the user choosing
Quit from an in-game menu.


@subsection moving_hints Persistent window hints

The `rblxwinOpenWindowHint` function has been renamed to @ref rblxwinWindowHint.

Window hints are no longer reset to their default values on window creation, but
instead retain their values until modified by @ref rblxwinWindowHint or @ref
rblxwinDefaultWindowHints, or until the library is terminated and re-initialized.


@subsection moving_video_modes Video mode enumeration

Video mode enumeration is now per-monitor.  The @ref rblxwinGetVideoModes function
now returns all available modes for a specific monitor instead of requiring you
to guess how large an array you need.  The `rblxwinGetDesktopMode` function, which
had poorly defined behavior, has been replaced by @ref rblxwinGetVideoMode, which
returns the current mode of a monitor.


@subsection moving_char_up Removal of character actions

The action parameter of the [character callback](@ref RBLXWINcharfun) has been
removed.  This was an artefact of the origin of RBLXWIN, i.e. being developed in
English by a Swede.  However, many keyboard layouts require more than one key to
produce characters with diacritical marks. Even the Swedish keyboard layout
requires this for uncommon cases like ü.

@par Old syntax
@code
void RBLXWINCALL character_callback(int character, int action);
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
void character_callback(RBLXWINwindow* window, int character);
@endcode


@subsection moving_cursorpos Cursor position changes

The `rblxwinGetMousePos` function has been renamed to @ref rblxwinGetCursorPos,
`rblxwinSetMousePos` to @ref rblxwinSetCursorPos and `rblxwinSetMousePosCallback` to @ref
rblxwinSetCursorPosCallback.

The cursor position is now `double` instead of `int`, both for the direct
functions and for the callback.  Some platforms can provide sub-pixel cursor
movement and this data is now passed on to the application where available.  On
platforms where this is not provided, the decimal part is zero.

RBLXWIN 3 only allows you to position the cursor within a window using @ref
rblxwinSetCursorPos (formerly `rblxwinSetMousePos`) when that window is active.
Unless the window is active, the function fails silently.


@subsection moving_wheel Wheel position replaced by scroll offsets

The `rblxwinGetMouseWheel` function has been removed.  Scrolling is the input of
offsets and has no absolute position.  The mouse wheel callback has been
replaced by a [scroll callback](@ref RBLXWINscrollfun) that receives
two-dimensional floating point scroll offsets.  This allows you to receive
precise scroll data from for example modern touchpads.

@par Old syntax
@code
void RBLXWINCALL mouse_wheel_callback(int position);
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
void scroll_callback(RBLXWINwindow* window, double xoffset, double yoffset);
@endcode

@par Removed functions
`rblxwinGetMouseWheel`


@subsection moving_repeat Key repeat action

The `RBLXWIN_KEY_REPEAT` enable has been removed and key repeat is always enabled
for both keys and characters.  A new key action, `RBLXWIN_REPEAT`, has been added
to allow the [key callback](@ref RBLXWINkeyfun) to distinguish an initial key press
from a repeat.  Note that @ref rblxwinGetKey still returns only `RBLXWIN_PRESS` or
`RBLXWIN_RELEASE`.


@subsection moving_keys Physical key input

RBLXWIN 3 key tokens map to physical keys, unlike in RBLXWIN 2 where they mapped to
the values generated by the current keyboard layout.  The tokens are named
according to the values they would have using the standard US layout, but this
is only a convenience, as most programmers are assumed to know that layout.
This means that (for example) `RBLXWIN_KEY_LEFT_BRACKET` is always a single key and
is the same key in the same place regardless of what keyboard layouts the users
of your program has.

The key input facility was never meant for text input, although using it that
way worked slightly better in RBLXWIN 2.  If you were using it to input text, you
should be using the character callback instead, on both RBLXWIN 2 and 3.  This will
give you the characters being input, as opposed to the keys being pressed.

RBLXWIN 3 has key tokens for all keys on a standard 105 key keyboard, so instead of
having to remember whether to check for `a` or `A`, you now check for
@ref RBLXWIN_KEY_A.


@subsection moving_joystick Joystick function changes

The `rblxwinGetJoystickPos` function has been renamed to @ref rblxwinGetJoystickAxes.

The `rblxwinGetJoystickParam` function and the `RBLXWIN_PRESENT`, `RBLXWIN_AXES` and
`RBLXWIN_BUTTONS` tokens have been replaced by the @ref rblxwinJoystickPresent
function as well as axis and button counts returned by the @ref
rblxwinGetJoystickAxes and @ref rblxwinGetJoystickButtons functions.


@subsection moving_mbcs Win32 MBCS support

The Win32 port of RBLXWIN 3 will not compile in
[MBCS mode](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/5z097dxa.aspx).
However, because the use of the Unicode version of the Win32 API doesn't affect
the process as a whole, but only those windows created using it, it's perfectly
possible to call MBCS functions from other parts of the same application.
Therefore, even if an application using RBLXWIN has MBCS mode code, there's no need
for RBLXWIN itself to support it.


@subsection moving_windows Support for versions of Windows older than XP

All explicit support for version of Windows older than XP has been removed.
There is no code that actively prevents RBLXWIN 3 from running on these earlier
versions, but it uses Win32 functions that those versions lack.

Windows XP was released in 2001, and by now (January 2015) it has not only
replaced almost all earlier versions of Windows, but is itself rapidly being
replaced by Windows 7 and 8.  The MSDN library doesn't even provide
documentation for version older than Windows 2000, making it difficult to
maintain compatibility with these versions even if it was deemed worth the
effort.

The Win32 API has also not stood still, and RBLXWIN 3 uses many functions only
present on Windows XP or later.  Even supporting an OS as new as XP (new
from the perspective of RBLXWIN 2, which still supports Windows 95) requires
runtime checking for a number of functions that are present only on modern
version of Windows.


@subsection moving_syskeys Capture of system-wide hotkeys

The ability to disable and capture system-wide hotkeys like Alt+Tab has been
removed.  Modern applications, whether they're games, scientific visualisations
or something else, are nowadays expected to be good desktop citizens and allow
these hotkeys to function even when running in full screen mode.


@subsection moving_terminate Automatic termination

RBLXWIN 3 does not register @ref rblxwinTerminate with `atexit` at initialization,
because `exit` calls registered functions from the calling thread and while it
is permitted to call `exit` from any thread, @ref rblxwinTerminate must only be
called from the main thread.

To release all resources allocated by RBLXWIN, you should call @ref rblxwinTerminate
yourself, from the main thread, before the program terminates.  Note that this
destroys all windows not already destroyed with @ref rblxwinDestroyWindow,
invalidating any window handles you may still have.


@subsection moving_glu GLU header inclusion

RBLXWIN 3 does not by default include the GLU header and GLU itself has been
deprecated by [Khronos](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khronos_Group).  __New
projects should not use GLU__, but if you need it for legacy code that
has been moved to RBLXWIN 3, you can request that the RBLXWIN header includes it by
defining @ref RBLXWIN_INCLUDE_GLU before the inclusion of the RBLXWIN header.

@par Old syntax
@code
#include <GL/rblxwin.h>
@endcode

@par New syntax
@code
#define RBLXWIN_INCLUDE_GLU
#include <RBLXWIN/rblxwin.h>
@endcode

There are many libraries that offer replacements for the functionality offered
by GLU.  For the matrix helper functions, see math libraries like
[RBLXMATH](https://github.com/g-truc/rblxmath) (for C++),
[linmath.h](https://github.com/datenwolf/linmath.h) (for C) and others.  For the
tessellation functions, see for example
[libtess2](https://github.com/memononen/libtess2).


@section moving_tables Name change tables


@subsection moving_renamed_functions Renamed functions

| RBLXWIN 2                      | RBLXWIN 3                        | Notes |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----- |
| `rblxwinOpenWindow`            | @ref rblxwinCreateWindow         | All channel bit depths are now hints
| `rblxwinCloseWindow`           | @ref rblxwinDestroyWindow        |       |
| `rblxwinOpenWindowHint`        | @ref rblxwinWindowHint           | Now accepts all `RBLXWIN_*_BITS` tokens |
| `rblxwinEnable`                | @ref rblxwinSetInputMode         |       |
| `rblxwinDisable`               | @ref rblxwinSetInputMode         |       |
| `rblxwinGetMousePos`           | @ref rblxwinGetCursorPos         |       |
| `rblxwinSetMousePos`           | @ref rblxwinSetCursorPos         |       |
| `rblxwinSetMousePosCallback`   | @ref rblxwinSetCursorPosCallback |       |
| `rblxwinSetMouseWheelCallback` | @ref rblxwinSetScrollCallback    | Accepts two-dimensional scroll offsets as doubles |
| `rblxwinGetJoystickPos`        | @ref rblxwinGetJoystickAxes      |       |
| `rblxwinGetWindowParam`        | @ref rblxwinGetWindowAttrib      |       |
| `rblxwinGetGLVersion`          | @ref rblxwinGetWindowAttrib      | Use `RBLXWIN_CONTEXT_VERSION_MAJOR`, `RBLXWIN_CONTEXT_VERSION_MINOR` and `RBLXWIN_CONTEXT_REVISION` |
| `rblxwinGetDesktopMode`        | @ref rblxwinGetVideoMode         | Returns the current mode of a monitor |
| `rblxwinGetJoystickParam`      | @ref rblxwinJoystickPresent      | The axis and button counts are provided by @ref rblxwinGetJoystickAxes and @ref rblxwinGetJoystickButtons |


@subsection moving_renamed_types Renamed types

| RBLXWIN 2              | RBLXWIN 3                | Notes |
| ------------------- | --------------------- |       |
| `RBLXWINmousewheelfun` | @ref RBLXWINscrollfun    |       |
| `RBLXWINmouseposfun`   | @ref RBLXWINcursorposfun |       |


@subsection moving_renamed_tokens Renamed tokens

| RBLXWIN 2                      | RBLXWIN 3                       | Notes |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ----- |
| `RBLXWIN_OPENGL_VERSION_MAJOR` | `RBLXWIN_CONTEXT_VERSION_MAJOR` | Renamed as it applies to OpenGL ES as well |
| `RBLXWIN_OPENGL_VERSION_MINOR` | `RBLXWIN_CONTEXT_VERSION_MINOR` | Renamed as it applies to OpenGL ES as well |
| `RBLXWIN_FSAA_SAMPLES`         | `RBLXWIN_SAMPLES`               | Renamed to match the OpenGL API |
| `RBLXWIN_ACTIVE`               | `RBLXWIN_FOCUSED`               | Renamed to match the window focus callback |
| `RBLXWIN_WINDOW_NO_RESIZE`     | `RBLXWIN_RESIZABLE`             | The default has been inverted |
| `RBLXWIN_MOUSE_CURSOR`         | `RBLXWIN_CURSOR`                | Used with @ref rblxwinSetInputMode |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_ESC`              | `RBLXWIN_KEY_ESCAPE`            |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_DEL`              | `RBLXWIN_KEY_DELETE`            |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_PAGEUP`           | `RBLXWIN_KEY_PAGE_UP`           |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_PAGEDOWN`         | `RBLXWIN_KEY_PAGE_DOWN`         |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_KP_NUM_LOCK`      | `RBLXWIN_KEY_NUM_LOCK`          |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_LCTRL`            | `RBLXWIN_KEY_LEFT_CONTROL`      |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_LSHIFT`           | `RBLXWIN_KEY_LEFT_SHIFT`        |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_LALT`             | `RBLXWIN_KEY_LEFT_ALT`          |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_LSUPER`           | `RBLXWIN_KEY_LEFT_SUPER`        |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_RCTRL`            | `RBLXWIN_KEY_RIGHT_CONTROL`     |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_RSHIFT`           | `RBLXWIN_KEY_RIGHT_SHIFT`       |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_RALT`             | `RBLXWIN_KEY_RIGHT_ALT`         |       |
| `RBLXWIN_KEY_RSUPER`           | `RBLXWIN_KEY_RIGHT_SUPER`       |       |

*/
